# Policy Bundle Manifests

Every subdirectory directly under `policies/` is built into an independent
OPA bundle by `.github/workflows/bundle.yaml` (`opa build -b policies/<dir>`).

**When creating a new subdirectory under `policies/`** (a new policy
package), you MUST also add a `<dir>/.manifest` file declaring `roots`
scoped to that package's `package` name(s), e.g.:

```json
{
  "roots": ["my_new_policy"]
}
```

- `roots` must match the Rego `package` name(s) used by the non-test files
  in that directory (check with `rg '^package ' policies/<dir>/*.rego`,
  excluding `*_test.rego` files).
- Do this immediately when scaffolding the directory, not as an
  afterthought — don't wait to be asked.

## Why this matters

Without a `.manifest`, `opa build` defaults to `"roots": [""]`, meaning the
bundle claims the entire data document. On every bundle activation, OPA
erases all data under those roots that isn't part of the bundle — including
unrelated locally-mounted policies like `k8s/base/rego/system/authz.rego`
(package `system`).

This exact gap caused a production incident: the `autodotes_policy` bundle
had no `.manifest`, so each bundle activation wiped `data.system.authz`,
making every OPA request (including `/health` liveness/readiness probes)
return `500 authorization policy missing or undefined` and crash-loop the
pod. See `policies/autodotes_policy/.manifest` for the reference fix.
